"[2511.09146] DoPE: Denoising Rotary Position Embedding (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09146)\nciteturn5925view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09146\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 6 Jan 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:DoPE: Denoising Rotary Position Embedding\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Jing Xiong , cite7†Liyang Fan , cite8†Hui Shen , cite9†Zunhai Su , cite10†Min Yang , cite11†Lingpeng Kong , cite12†Ngai Wong L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled DoPE: Denoising Rotary Position Embedding, by Jing Xiong and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Positional encoding is essential for large language models (LLMs) to represent sequence order, yet recent studies show that Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE) can induce massive activation. We investigate the source of these instabilities via a spectral analysis of RoPE, and show that its low-frequency components concentrate structured energy, producing low-rank, over-aligned attention patterns.\nL17: We theoretically reveal that this low-frequency alignment manifests as activation noise, degrading stability during long-context extrapolation. To mitigate this effect, we introduce Denoising Rotary Position Embedding (DoPE), a training-free method that identifies and suppresses noisy attention heads using truncated matrix entropy, then reparameterizes their attention maps with an isotropic Gaussian distribution.\nL18: Across a range of settings, DoPE improves length extrapolation performance without fine-tuning, increases robustness to perturbations, and boosts both needle-in-a-haystack and many-shot in-context learning tasks. These results suggest that selective positional encoding is key to robust extrapolation. Our project page is Project: cite15†this https URL†the-physical-picture-of-llms.github.io L19: Comments:  | Technical Report\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2511.09146 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2511.09146v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09146†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jing Xiong [cite19†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 09:32:35 UTC (5,747 KB)\nL28: [v2] Tue, 6 Jan 2026 14:02:29 UTC (2,029 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled DoPE: Denoising Rotary Position Embedding, by Jing Xiong and 6 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite13†View PDF L37:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite20†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L46: \nL47: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite27†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite28†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite31†What is the Explorer?†in